        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-cd59e8d220d58baf\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"Roxane directly avows that she loves the person she believes she is addressing. The coordinated declaration “et suis tienne” (“and I am yours”) also gives the exchange substantial relational-undertaking force. Her trembling, tears, and intoxication establish intense affective delivery but do not independently show that “je t’aime” is a reflex-like verbal discharge rather than a passionate avowal and undertaking.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The performative force depends partly on whether “I am yours” is heard as an enacted commitment or as a figurative description of emotional surrender; either reading leaves T dominant.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Roxane speaks the declaration directly from the balcony to the man she takes to be Christian, although Cyrano’s concealed speaking has produced the response and both men hear it.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Roxane responds to Cyrano’s impassioned declaration while believing its words and voice belong to Christian. Her love avowal is sincere as presented, but its intended object is bound up with mistaken authorship: she attributes Cyrano’s eloquence and emotional disclosure to Christian. The deception and dramatic irony complicate whom she understands herself to love, without changing the utterance’s core avowal and relational-undertaking force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Oui, je tremble, et je pleure, et je t’aime, et suis tienne !”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense “je t’aime” strongly supports T; “suis tienne” supplies substantial P-like relational commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane says that she trembles and weeps and that the addressee has intoxicated her.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes intense emotional involvement and delivery, but does not by itself establish independent expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Cyrano has spoken the love declaration from concealment, while Christian remains beneath the balcony; Christian then urges taking advantage of how deeply moved Roxane is.\",\"supports\":\"Shows the mistaken-authorship structure and dramatic manipulation surrounding Roxane’s otherwise direct avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane identifies the preceding speech as genuine love—“Oui, c’est bien de l’amour”—before making her reciprocal declaration.\",\"supports\":\"Supports interpreting her “je t’aime” as a considered reciprocal recognition and avowal rather than a semantically ungrounded token.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the explicit avowal, and P captures the accompanying declaration of belonging or commitment. Emotional intensity and the mistaken identity are adequately handled as contextual features, so neither E nor O is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
